SM(279,{"n":"Exodus 2","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Exodus 2.mp3","d":2408.9,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.12,"Chapter 2, verse 1."],[24.0,"Now a man of the house of Levi went and took as a wife, a daughter of Levi."],[30.16,"So the woman conceived and bore a son."],[32.8,"And see he was a beautiful child, they hid him for three months."],[36.4,"The Hebrew literally says he was a good child."],[38.8,"But the idea is he's healthy."],[42.26,"He's a healthy everything child, and they but they say, uh-oh."],[49.06,"It's open season on boy babies."],[51.96,"So they hide him for three months after his birth."],[58.12,"But when she could no longer hide him she took an arc of bulrushes, daubed it with asphalt."],[66.56,"Asphalt. Anyway, it's tar or pitch."],[72.48,"She's not paving a street."],[73.92,"Anyway, put the child in it and laid it in the reeds by the river's bank."],[80.48,"So here's already another, describing this basket as an ark, describing it being sealed with pitch and tar."],[88.97,"Think back to Noah."],[93.42,"Now his sister was watching from a distance to learn what would be done with him."],[98.78,"Okay, so she goes and says because she can't she can't hide him anymore."],[102.94,"So verse 5"],[103.22,"He's making noise."],[104.26,"He's right. Eventually he's gonna get found and potentially killed."],[109.3,"Then the daughter of Pharaoh came down to bathe at the river, and her maidens walked along the riverside, and seeing the ark among the reeds, she sent a maid to get it."],[118.9,"So when she opened the ark, she saw the child crying, and the daughter of Pharaoh had compassion on him, and said, This is one of the Hebrews' children."],[128.58,"Okay, so she's aware it's an Israelite baby."],[133.78,"How? Yes. What?"],[138.18,"Circumcision. Oh. Yeah, that's probably a very easy way to shake."],[142.66,"Yeah. But this is gonna become an issue later with Moses."],[147.06,"That circumcision. But we'll talk about that more when we get to it"],[151.02,"So she recognizes a Hebrew baby, but she says, you know, having some kind of decency says, I'm not going to drown it."],[161.8,"This baby. Then his sister said to Pharaoh's daughter, Shall I go and call a nurse for you from the Hebrew women that she may nurse the child for you?"],[170.38,"So Pharaoh's daughter is going to adopt this baby, but not to be overly blunt, but she's not lactating because she didn't have a baby."],[179.26,"And so, little Hebrew girl, who Pharaoh's daughter doesn't know is related, says, Hey, I can go and get you a Hebrew woman who is a nursing mother. to come and nurse this baby for you as a wet nurse"],[198.04,"So Pharaoh's daughter said to her, Go, then the maiden went and called the child's mother."],[203.89,"So it goes against Moses' mother, who gave birth to Moses, and so is ready to nurse."],[211.49,"Pharaoh's daughter then said to her, Take this child and nurse him for me, and I will pay you."],[216.53,"So the woman took the child and nursed him."],[219.89,"And when the boy was grown, she brought him to Pharaoh's daughter, and he became her son."],[224.69,"And she called his name Moses, saying, Because I drew him out of the water."],[229.25,"So this is interesting."],[231.45,"Because, as we just mentioned, that's not what Moses means in Egyptian."],[235.73,"That's trying to find a Hebrew etymology for Moses"],[241.76,"Which is kind of close to something that means like drawn out or pulled out."],[246.64,"So it says pulled up out of the water."],[248.8,"In the Hebrew, the phrasing there is interesting because it has both."],[254.24,"It says, Because I'm gonna call him Moses because I pulled a child out of the water."],[258.96,"So Moses means child in Egyptian."],[262.4,"And in Hebrew, it's sort of close to the word drew out of the water."],[265.44,"So there's a little kind of a wordplay there where it includes both."],[271.6,"So there is a question here that is ultimately not a resolvable question."],[277.44,"But I lean toward what Zide, which I'll tell you. but we can't prove for sure"],[283.08,"And that is was Moses his whole name?"],[287.32,"Or was there the name of an Egyptian god in front of it?"],[292.12,"That got dropped because, of course, he's gonna become a prophet of Yahweh, the God of Israel."],[299.29,"As I said, there's no way to prove that."],[303.55,"One way or other, for sure."],[305.15,"It is certainly possible."],[308.36,"And it would make sense for Pharaoh's daughter to give him a name that included the name of an Egyptian god, and it would also make sense to drop that."],[319.16,"But here's why I don't think that's the case."],[322.52,"Because of his sister's name."],[326.4,"His sister's name is usually in English transliterated as Miriam."],[333.52,"It's actually Miriam, which is also an Egyptian name."],[340.24,"And Miriamni is from means beloved of Amun."],[346.72,"So she has a pagan god's name in her name."],[350.96,"And it doesn't get taken out in the rest of the Torah, in the rest of the Pentateuch."],[357.4,"And so for that reason, I don't think if Moses had had uh an Egyptian god in his name, I don't think they would have taken it out since they didn't take it out of her name."],[369.24,"Aaron's name is also an Egyptian name."],[371.64,"Now you may be wondering, why would these other kids of the same family who are not adopted by Pharaoh have Egyptian names?"],[380.36,"You try and get rid of them."],[381.8,"You try to assimilate them?"],[383.32,"Well, but she's a daughter."],[385.0,"Yeah, Mariana, you could assimilate assimilate her, try to marry off to some."],[388.9,"But why would her parents that?"],[392.02,"There's nothing saying they did."],[395.67,"We're gonna see that it's not just this family."],[401.11,"A whole pile of the Levites who get named in the Torah have Egyptian names."],[410.97,"Have Egyptian names."],[411.93,"So we're gonna be talking about this all through the rest of the Torah through the rest of the Pedatech"],[418.29,"But this is one of the first places we see it."],[421.17,"That the people who are gonna become the original nation of Israel are not ethnically united."],[429.81,"They're not literally biologically descended from Abraham."],[434.69,"All of them."],[435.49,"Some of them are."],[437.25,"But a lot of them aren't."],[440.02,"Because that's not how tribe and clan identity worked in the ancient world."],[444.74,"They didn't know that DNA existed."],[446.63,"That's a 20th century discovery."],[449.03,"There was no 23 in me."],[451.11,"You couldn't demonstrate."],[452.73,"You couldn't even demonstrate paternity in terms of who you're immediate father was for sure in the ancient world."],[455.37,"at. Let alone who your gre great great great great great great great grandfather was"],[463.41,"So identity in these tribes and clans is something that happened through rituals."],[473.57,"Through the rituals that made up the life of the tribes and the clans."],[478.29,"Meaning what God they worshipped."],[488.65,"Participating in the in the the clan, the family of the clan, the tribal unit."],[495.21,"You could marry into them, you could become a servant in one, and now you're part of the household."],[499.37,"So the servants in a Levite household were considered Levites"],[505.23,"Regardless of their quote unquote ethnicity in modern terms."],[514.67,"The first sort of tease of this was actually with Joseph marrying the daughter of an Egyptian priest."],[521.83,"That this is this this whole idea of we have to get this out of our head."],[526.07,"Because it's not what the Torah says."],[533.57,"These people are all not ethnically, are not all ethnically related to each other."],[539.01,"And this is, as we go through the Torah, we're gonna see more and more extreme examples."],[546.07,"Like, just to tease it, the third high priest of Israel is black."],[551.27,"He's literally an African."],[554.71,"So there is not."],[557.05,"We think of Jewish as an ethnicity, and so we assume that the original Israel was literally the people who they're descended from."],[570.89,"In some cases that's true."],[572.73,"Some cases not so much."],[573.93,"There are people who converted along the way."],[575.39,"There are people who."],[576.31,"But also, once Israel is formed, and then once Israel goes into the land and lives in the land, well then, yes, Israelites are going to start marrying other Israelites"],[590.2,"People in a village are gonna marry other people in the village."],[593.53,"And so over time, there's gonna be such a thing as an ethnic Israelite."],[601.55,"Once you get five, six, seven generations in of people intermarrying."],[606.65,"But at this first level, at the beginning of Israel, and we're not even there yet."],[612.4,"Nascent Israel here is ethnically diverse in our modern terms."],[618.96,"Okay. So the reason I put so much emphasis on this is that a lot of people that study the Bible have been given this false narrative that the story of the Torah, the story of the Old Testament, even sometimes salvation is this ethnic thing in the Old Testament, and then in the New Testament it's not."],[644.6,"And the truth is it never was."],[650.81,"And actually, the early church is very parallel to this."],[656.01,"The early church was ethnically diverse, but then what happens?"],[659.61,"Christians start marrying other Christians."],[663.69,"In different places in the world."],[665.53,"They're not going to marry pagans"],[668.75,"Later on, they're not going to marry Muslims."],[670.93,"Christians are going to marry. other Christians"],[672.97,"And so, in various places, the Christian churches are going to become more ethnically uniform than they were when St."],[680.97,"Paul went and established them."],[684.25,"But the same is true of Israel originally."],[688.09,"It's ethnically diverse at this first layer."],[690.41,"And so probably a lot of these Levites, maybe even Moses and Aaron and Miriamni, were might have been ethnically Egyptian."],[704.51,"By those terms."],[708.11,"But they're considered because their family unit is part of a clan that's part of the tribe of Levi, they're considered Le.vites and they're considered Hebrews and they're considered Israelites"],[719.03,"It doesn't matter what color their skin is, who their parents were, you know, five generations back."],[728.95,"Wow, then why are they still so suspicious of them being like Asiatics?"],[732.87,"Is it just the because they're not ritually participating?"],[735.75,"Because those clan bonds, they don't have citizenship."],[739.48,"Your loyalties were to your family and your clan and your tribe and your kin."],[746.49,"So they weren't participating in the world."],[748.01,"So these these people are have closer kinship ties, ritually and everything, to like the Edomites, who worship the same god they do."],[757.13,"Even though they have Egypt and the Midianites."],[759.21,"Which would suggest that they participated in some kind of Egyptian."],[759.85,"Okay Well, those might have been handed down from their great grandfather."],[767.09,"Or you might even have a situation too where it's kind of like uh what what's this version of your name in the Egyptian kind of thing happening, maybe."],[776.77,"Yeah, but especially when it's the when you have the name of an Egyptian god in there."],[781.65,"Yeah. That's kind of that's kind of telling."],[786.98,"Or if you're a slave in an Egyptian household, you might have your name."],[791.22,"And we also have to consider what is the way of life of Israelites at this point."],[797.54,"Slavery. The re well, but the rest of the Torah is gonna lay out here's what it means to be Israel."],[802.42,"Israel Here's how theites are going to eat."],[803.06,"Here is how they are going to dress"],[803.26,"Yeah. Here is how they are going to conduct themselves."],[810.91,"Here are the feasts they're going to celebrate."],[812.51,"Here's how they're going to offer sacrifices."],[814.75,"They haven't received any of that yet."],[817.07,"So in circumcision, how do you That's right?"],[820.83,"So there's not a, at this point, a ra as radical a difference in their way of life from the Egyptian way of life."],[832.85,"As there will be later."],[835.33,"There's not necessarily this sense of antipathy."],[838.69,"We talked about that a little when he talked about Joseph marrying the daughter of an Egyptian priest."],[843.41,"Like for the perspective of like Ezra and Nehemiah after the exile."],[847.73,"The whole thing is you can't have foreign wives"],[853.83,"And we even saw some of that in Genesis."],[856.81,"Like with Esau married Canaanites."],[858.65,"It wasn't foreign, it was Canaanites."],[861.39,"That this is this big problem."],[863.47,"And then the text just sort of casually tells us that Joseph marries the daughter of a pagan priest."],[869.07,"And we talked about how these stories about Joseph and Asenath, his wife, get structured out of the question of wait, what?"],[878.13,"To sort of explain that and talk about her conversion and her right becoming an Israelite and all this."],[884.57,"So the people haven't really been called out of Egypt in the same way."],[891.33,"They're worshiping a particular god, uh, and they're not supposed to be worshiping the Egyptian gods."],[900.26,"But we saw like with Laban, Abraham's uh brother in law and stuff that other things were happening."],[910.73,"We saw with uh even with Judah, like some of Jacob's son, they were participating in some of this stuff, so there isn't this, there isn't as as clear a distinction in way of life yet."],[925.12,"So the question from the Egyptian side is loyalties."],[929.22,"If they have to choose between fighting for Egypt or fighting for their cousins from the East, who are they going to choose?"],[939.26,"I'm worried it's gonna be right their cousins from the East."],[943.52,"Why would they side with us?"],[948.65,"But where they don't, they haven't yet been given this radically."],[953.87,"They're not they're not wearing tassels on their garments yet."],[957.31,"They're not right."],[959.23,"Um so as far as them retaining their kind of tribal identity related to the son of Jacob that they're descended from, or at least named after."],[973.87,"Yeah. Yeah. how would I mean have just this is a distant memory, so we're part of this tribe."],[974.71,"How would they?"],[979.67,"It's the continuity of the family traditions."],[982.52,"And telling the stories of their ancestor."],[985.72,"Without a way to check and see, like, is he really our ancestor?"],[989.48,"Well, he's our ancestor because we're part of the and they were part of the unit now."],[995.56,"Manage that even in spite of being like enslaved in that."],[999.74,"But it would just be oral tradition at this point."],[1003.1,"There's like in America."],[1005.1,"I mean, how many of us are descended from the 13 colonies?"],[1009.5,"Oh, we're descending all kinds of different people."],[1013.1,"So we think of ourselves as being founded by George Washington and those people."],[1018.9,"So we've sort of adopted the founding fathers as our founding fathers."],[1026.82,"People have the family history of like, you know, I had whatever ancestor from whatever a Native American tribe."],[1033.22,"Now that's something orally passed down, but like genetically, no one actually knows."],[1037.46,"And we talked about, remember, we talked about with the altars and the patriarchal narratives?"],[1042.02,"How what you have pulled together there in the patriarchal narratives is sort of the traditions of each of these groups regarding what would come to be their piece of land."],[1058.15,"And their forefather who was there had built this altar at this place."],[1062.57,"Sort of all of those traditions are brought together."],[1065.55,"But so they haven't been brought together yet."],[1068.12,"At this point, they're all these separate."],[1070.06,"They all trace their line back to Abraham, but so as we're going to see to a bunch of like Midianites and Edomites and Ishmaelites."],[1079.52,"They're going to say, yeah, Abraham's our ancestor too."],[1081.92,"And the Moabites and the Ammonites are going to say, yeah, Abraham's our ancestor too."],[1089.44,"They're going to have their own sort of set of traditions."],[1093.36,"And through that storytelling and being passed on, that becomes."],[1096.96,"And that's even true in the New Testament."],[1099.28,"We saw that in 1 Corinthians, remember."],[1101.52,"St. Paul at one point is very clearly talking to Gentiles."],[1105.76,"He's very clearly talking to people who, you know, a couple of years ago were pagans."],[1106.48,"And who are now Christians"],[1113.62,"And he says to them, Remember, our fathers went through the Red Sea with Moses."],[1120.1,"And you're like, you know."],[1124.18,"I don't think most Greeks living in Corinth had any ancestors there."],[1131.67,"But that's not how St."],[1133.11,"Paul is thinking about it."],[1136.48,"Those are their fathers now."],[1139.76,"Because they've become Christians."],[1142.48,"So, and that's a big key."],[1144.4,"We saw that over and over again with what St."],[1145.92,"Paul was saying in like Galatians and Romans."],[1148.34,"Abraham is your father if you are faithful."],[1153.63,"And if someone is unfaithful, it doesn't matter if, according to the flesh, is how he says it."],[1158.75,"But we would say genetically."],[1160.03,"It doesn't matter if genetically Abraham is your ancestor if you're faithless."],[1166.56,"What makes you his child, his descendant is being faithful like he was."],[1179.28,"Okay. So Moses is now adopted by and growing up within Pharaoh's extended household."],[1189.6,"Which means his life is very different than the other Israelites."],[1197.8,"Thus it came to pass in those days when Moses was grown, that he went out to his brethren and saw their burdens."],[1204.68,"He also saw an Egyptian beating a Hebrew, one of his brethren of the sons of Israel."],[1210.2,"So he looked this way and that, and when he saw no one, he killed the Egyptian and hid him in the sand."],[1219.64,"Moses sees this, he gets angry."],[1224.06,"He kills the Egyptian."],[1226.46,"Hides the body."],[1228.3,"Okay So this is one of the first things we're seeing about Moses himself."],[1235.03,"Other than that he was a cute, healthy baby."],[1236.79,"But about his personality is he may have some anger management issues"],[1242.65,"This will play out later in his story."],[1245.15,"Later in his life."],[1246.75,"But what's the big problem here?"],[1250.03,"Because you think."],[1250.93,"I mean, he's one of Pharaoh's adopted kids, basically."],[1254.15,"He's like Pharaoh's adopted grandson."],[1255.99,"You could probably get away with killing some rando Egyptian."],[1262.55,"Well, one of the biggest problems is again the loyalty issue."],[1267.03,"It's he killed an Egyptian to protect one of these Asiatics."],[1273.91,"So, where are Moses' loyalties?"],[1278.79,"Is he loyal to Pharaoh? who's been bringing him up in his home and to the Egyptians"],[1283.55,"Or is he loyal to these other people?"],[1288.03,"When he went out the second day, he saw two Hebrew men fighting."],[1291.95,"And he said to the one who did the wrong, why are you hitting your neighbor?"],[1295.71,"So the one who did the wrong, the one who was winning, the one who was beating up the other one."],[1300.43,"He said, Why are you beating up your neighbor, your fellow Israelite?"],[1305.07,"Why would you do this?"],[1308.03,"He replied, Who made you a prince and a judge over us?"],[1311.47,"Do you intend to kill me as you killed the Egyptian?"],[1316.27,"So Moses was alarmed and said, Surely this thing is known."],[1321.72,"Oh no, people know what happened."],[1323.24,"L likeike, I didn't do such a good job of you know."],[1324.68,"of. I mean, burying him in the sand, that is kind of the definition a shallow grave"],[1330.38,"And I don't know why people do that, but I won't digress on this too much."],[1333.58,"If you're a murderer, and don't be a murderer, but why do murderers always bury people in shallow graves?"],[1339.26,"Like it seems like they're gonna find the body, like, you know, but anyway."],[1345.66,"Uh so crocking those."],[1348.46,"Moses says, I've found out."],[1350.08,"This is gonna be bad."],[1351.34,"When this gets back to Pharaoh."],[1354.78,"And sure enough, verse 15, thus when Pharaoh heard this matter, he sought to kill Moses."],[1361.26,"But Moses fled from the face of Pharaoh and dwelt in the land of Midian, and he sat down by a well."],[1367.02,"Okay, so Moses takes off."],[1368.66,"He takes off to the east into the desert."],[1371.62,"We're going to find out."],[1374.82,"But just to set this in your mind."],[1377.54,"Moses in the Hebrew numbers lives to be 120."],[1382.74,"And his life is divided into four periods of 40 years each."],[1389.54,"So all we got was this."],[1392.42,"We got twice, we got, okay, he's nursed by his actual mother, his birth mother, until he was grown."],[1400.1,"And then we read later when he was grown."],[1403.06,"Also, so presumably the first one is when he was weaned."],[1407.4,"That's still a little vague."],[1408.6,"We don't know exactly how old he was."],[1410.76,"But then when he was grown."],[1412.68,"But we're gonna find out he's 40 at this point"],[1420.4,"So he has lived a good chunk of life."],[1422.8,"Keep in mind in the ancient world and in Egypt here, when you hit puberty, so around 13, 14, was you're considered an adult."],[1430.48,"So he has a decent chunk of adulthood under his belt."],[1434.8,"This is why we're gonna read about Moses being educated in the ways of the Egyptians, growing up at Pharaoh's court and getting to that age."],[1443.0,"He would have he'd be literate."],[1446.65,"Probably literate not only in hieratic, the Egyptian writing, but we know that Pharaoh's court was engaged in active correspondence with other cultures to the east to negotiate treaties, trade, all of these things."],[1466.21,"And we have letters from the Pharaoh's court written on cuneiform tablets, because that's what they were using."],[1473.01,"So Moses would have been familiar with that, and likely also then with traditions and things from further east."],[1483.09,"Probably not much further east than that."],[1485.33,"I don't think he would have known about things going on in India."],[1488.71,"And that kind of thing."],[1489.51,"ing. And there wasn't a lot going on in Europe at the time that involved writ"],[1497.43,"But so he has, he is an adult, he is a highly educated adult for this time and place, in the world."],[1505.49,"But now he's fled out into the desert by himself."],[1509.97,"Land of Midian, this is where the Midianites live."],[1513.17,"Shockingly. Where is it?"],[1515.73,"This is the desert immediately."],[1518.69,"It's basically the Sinai Desert."],[1520.45,"Okay. Yeah. And they're just called Midianites because that's where they live."],[1526.77,"But these are nomadic Bedouins."],[1530.53,"These are Bedouin tribes who, for the most part, trace their forefathers for them, are either Ishmael or Edom, Esau, for the most part."],[1544.39,"So these are sort of cousins of the Israelites, who live nomadically, the way Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob did live nomadically in this region."],[1555.04,"When Jacob slash Israel took his family to Egypt, you remember we didn't read about any of the other extended Abrahamite family going there."],[1565.84,"We're going to find out more of what happened with them when we get to Deuteronomy, specifically chapter two."],[1571.28,"But for now, this is a group of them that are living, living as desert nomads."],[1578.16,"This is where he goes."],[1579.68,"When you're out in the desert, it behooves you to find a well."],[1584.8,"And so Moses does."],[1589.2,"Now the priests. of Midian had seven daughter, who fed the sheep of their father Jethro"],[1595.88,"And they came and drew water and filled the troughs to water their father's flock."],[1601.16,"Then the shepherds came and drove them away."],[1604.52,"But Moses stood up and helped them and watered their flock."],[1609.56,"So this is because these are people who are living as nomads and Bedouins."],[1615.46,"No one can really claim to own this well."],[1617.96,"This well's been built around a spring."],[1620.78,"And so whoever finds it wants to use it."],[1624.46,"So we have this person, Jethro, who we're told is a priest."],[1628.54,"This person is referred to by another name."],[1631.18,"We'll talk more about it when we get there later in the tour."],[1633.94,"It's not until the book of Numbers, so it's gonna be a while."],[1636.1,"But suffice to say for now, Jethro may be a title."],[1639.54,"That other name may be his actual name."],[1642.26,"There's pretty good evidence that Jethro is a title for priests among these tribes."],[1652.5,"Now, he would have been a priest, not in the sense that he has a temple somewhere or something, because they're nomads."],[1659.24,"But you got to think about Abraham, Isaac, Jacob offering sacrifices."],[1664.13,"Where are they building altars and offering sacrifices?"],[1667.49,"So this is something that Jethro would have done primarily for his extended family unit, but the fact that he's identified as a priest in this way means there also would have been other people from other clans and other tribes who came to him to sort of supervise larger feasts and celebrations and things."],[1688.35,"When that involved sacrifices to the god that they worship."],[1693.23,"So now his he sends his seven daughters to collect water from the well, because they need water for all kinds of things, watering their animals, water for themselves, etc."],[1703.15,"And when they come, they start getting the water, but then these shepherds show up and they want to water their flocks, and so they sort of chase, go to chase the women away."],[1711.71,"But uh Moses goes all Boy Scout astronaut and steps in and says, I will save the day, and uh drives off the shepherds uh so that the the daughters can. can uh get water"],[1724.55,"Now, does this scene around this well remind you of anybody?"],[1730.39,"Remember the servant?"],[1731.99,"Yeah, to go and get Rebecca for Isaac."],[1734.55,"Remember, he went to the well and helped them."],[1737.27,"So there's echoes again."],[1740.2,"Of this uh imagery."],[1742.28,"Verse 18, when they came to rule their father, he said to them, How is it you came so soon today?"],[1751.16,"They replied, An Egyptian delivered us from the shepherds, and he also drew enough water for us and watered our sheep."],[1758.6,"His rule of the other night for Jethro?"],[1761.16,"No. This is another person in the extended family."],[1766.6,"So. Jethro is the head, sort of the head of the household"],[1771.58,"So sort of everyone there is that's like the sons of Jacob."],[1774.94,"Okay. Like everyone in the household are they're sons of Jethro."],[1779.45,"This is their like immediate father."],[1781.05,"So they may have been his granddaughters, and this is their father."],[1783.63,"So he says, How did you get back so quick?"],[1786.53,"It's like, oh, we got help from this guy."],[1788.63,"He drove the shepherds off who messed with us and helped us uh get water."],[1793.51,"So he said to his daughters, and where is he?"],[1796.55,"Why have you left the man?"],[1797.99,"Call him that he may eat bread."],[1799.59,"So he's like, he came and helped you, and you didn't like invite him over for dinner."],[1803.05,"Like, hospitality, hello."],[1805.7,"Then Moses dwelt with the man and he gave Zipporahh his daugter to Moses."],[1808.34,"So the woman conceived and bore Moses a son, and he called his name Gersham"],[1815.34,"For he said, I have been a sojourner in a foreign land."],[1819.74,"So there's a big time lapse here."],[1822.4,"It's not like he shows up for dinner and you know, he says, You know, I like you."],[1828.32,"Marry my daughter."],[1829.62,"Right now. This is right, there's a big time lapse here."],[1833.16,"He comes for dinner, he ends up staying, becoming part of this household."],[1838.36,"He marries the one daughter, they have a son."],[1842.41,"And gur is the Hebrew word for a foreigner or a sojourner."],[1847.98,"Someone who's living in somewhere other than their own a resident alien in modern term."],[1855.0,"Verse 23. Now, in the course of time, it happened that the king of Egypt died."],[1860.76,"Then the children of Israel groaned because of their labors and cried out, and their cry came up to God because of their labors."],[1867.48,"So God heard their groaning and God remembered his covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob."],[1872.68,"Then God looked upon the children of Israel and was made known to them."],[1876.76,"So, more time lapse, right here."],[1880.6,"Moses is going to end up spending the second 40 years of his life out here with Jethro in the desert."],[1890.08,"So this is important because when we get to this next part, where Moses gets sent back to Egypt, spoilers, he's gonna be 80 years old."],[1902.24,"That's when all this starts for Moses."],[1906.34,"The story of Moses that we think of as the story of Moses."],[1909.3,"It's like chapter 3."],[1910.74,"So the story of Moses that runs from Exodus chapter 3 to the end of Deuteronomy is from age 80 to age 120."],[1923.44,"That's when all this."],[1925.04,"So he will already have sort of lived two lives."],[1927.76,"This one life in Egypt, this other life as a nomad shepherding."],[1932.98,"Sheep. And then he gets he gets called to this."],[1937.95,"With uh the Israelites."],[1940.47,"So when he named his son Gersham, is he talking about a sojourner in a foreign land?"],[1945.67,"Is he talking about it being an Egyptian in the desert, or is he talking about being in the desert?"],[1951.83,"Probably both. He's probably saying my whole life I've been like an outsider."],[1957.93,"I've never really been you know, I'm different."],[1964.41,"Yeah. Yeah. So then we get this language of you know, after Pharaoh dies, the Israelites cry out, and and like, oh, so this is the first time they complained to God in prayer."],[1979.13,"No, obviously not."],[1980.96,"Then you get the and God remembered his covenant with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob."],[1982.32,"That does not mean like before that he forgot"],[1986.76,"It's like, oh yeah, I knew."],[1990.68,"Man, they've been in Egypt a long time."],[1993.3,"That's that's not what it's saying."],[1995.99,"Remembered means, that that God brought it to mind in the sense that look what he does next, he reveals himself to them."],[2004.15,"The time comes where he's going to act, where he's gonna bring that covenant, those promises to fruition."],[2010.01,"It doesn't mean he forgot before and just now he remembered."],[2013.18,"But now this is gonna become a present reality for them."],[2016.5,"Not just a promise, not just a hope, not just a memory in their oral tradition, but this is gonna become a real thing, and the God who who they've been worshiping,'s been part of that oral tradition and that ancestral tradition, is about to become very that's the revealed part."],[2031.94,"Their God is about to become very real to them."],[2036.06,"Because we talked about how within these stories, these ancestral traditions that make up the patriarchal narratives that we read in Genesis, in their memory and in their stories, like God appeared in person to Abraham and walked with him and talked with him and ate with him."],[2055.51,"And then Isaac, God appeared to him multiple times."],[2059.11,"Jacob, God appeared to him multiple times."],[2061.75,"And then we saw with Joseph."],[2064.99,"Got a little more, it's now it's dreams."],[2067.11,"There's sort of a little level of remove"],[2071.77,"But what's being suggested here is that even though they've kept these ancestral traditions and this kind of thing, God hasn't been appearing to anybody."],[2081.69,"He hasn't even been sending dreams or visions to anybody."],[2084.89,"There haven't been any prophets yet."],[2089.85,"So part of what's happening here now, with God coming to act, is related to the faithfulness of the Israelites. in maintaining in maintaining these things"],[2110.29,"But we're also going to see they're not the only ones who maintained these things."],[2114.21,"The Midianites, like the family Moses living with, also maintained these things."],[2118.93,"They're still worshiping the God of Abraham."],[2120.45,"That's who Jethro is offering sacrifices to, not some other god."],[2126.37,"We're going to find other people, other people who have."],[2129.81,"Again, this is another category that we're going to see play out in the Torah."],[2134.69,"So we've already talked a little enough tonight about the ethnic diversity thing, with Israel."],[2139.98,"But also we have to understand that descendants of Abraham, people who receive the promises of Abraham in the Torah."],[2148.16,"Remember, the promise is nations, plural will come from you."],[2152.96,"All the nations will be blessed by you."],[2155.14,"These are parts of the promises."],[2157.26,"So often we've been taught wrongly to misinterpret this idea that because there is this single line that produces Israel."],[2173.2,"That goes Isaac, Jacob."],[2176.86,"And then we talked about how it gets a little interesting, in the next generation, but ultimately it's going to go to Judah, that Judah ultimately is going to produce the Messiah."],[2185.99,"There is this David, and then."],[2187.69,"There's going to be this single line that's going to produce the Messiah, that's going to produce Christ."],[2193.42,"That doesn't mean that, like, those are the only people who are quote unquote saved."],[2200.5,"It doesn't mean that only those people inherit the promises to Abraham, because as we talked about, there's two elements to the promises to Abraham."],[2208.9,"There's a singular element, that's gonna lead to the Messiah."],[2213.72,"There's also this collective, broader element of his descendants, plural."],[2219.96,"And so we're gonna see as we go through the Torah that the fact that Esau wasn't the one through who the line was gonna be calculated."],[2230.32,"Doesn't mean that Esau was evil or bad, as we saw when we read about Esau."],[2234.76,"Esau is arguably presented as a better person than Jacob."],[2239.13,"More forgiving, less conniving."],[2243.46,"And the Edomites are not just cut off."],[2246.68,"These Midianites. The Ishmaelites are not just cut off."],[2251.5,"They're a part of this story too, and a part of Abraham's inheritance."],[2255.1,"And we're going to see this continue to play out in the Torah also."],[2257.82,"So there's another category that we have to get in our head and get rid of this bad category that sees everything, all of this stuff in terms of salvation."],[2267.18,"Like somehow salvation was for ethnic Israelites."],[2270.06,"There weren't ethnic Israelites."],[2271.66,"And somehow salvation was for this one line and not for the other children."],[2279.28,"We have to get that out of our out of our heads."],[2283.52,"But we're going to see that continue to play out."],[2285.2,"But key here, and I think this is maybe where we end up"],[2291.72,"Yeah, this is where we're going to end up stopping for tonight."],[2294.04,"But because we're not going to have time to go through chapter 3."],[2297.72,"But God is going to reveal Himself to Moses on the holy mountain of these Midianite people."],[2314.25,"They're still out there worshiping him."],[2317.78,"Then this is where Moses is going to encounter God, and then he's going to go back to Egypt to represent God to the Israelites."],[2329.1,"And we'll talk more about that next time."],[2331.18,"Because this there's a whole tradition weaving its way, I'll just tease it here, weaving its way through the whole Old Testament, through the whole Hebrew Bible, about God actually."],[2346.14,"Where is God during this period when the Israelites are in Egypt?"],[2350.46,"And the answer that most of the Old Testament of the Hebrew Bible gives is Edom."],[2355.5,"It's Mount Ser."],[2359.34,"That's the place where he's being worshipped during this time."],[2362.14,"And he comes from there to go and get Israel and bring them to Canaan."],[2369.1,"But we'll talk more about that next time."],[2371.18,"Because next time we're going to get the burning bush."],[2373.94,"A lot of passages that may seem familiar, but as we read them in detail, there will be things that jump out, maybe."],[2381.14,"So uh but we'll end here for tonight."],[2383.22,"Thank you, everybody."],[2386.5,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2393.86,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2399.22,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com"]],"o":[{"t":21.12,"lv":2,"h":"Exodus 2:1-4 - A good child in an ark","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Levite man takes <b>daughter of Levi</b>; son born</li><li class=\"d0\">He was \"<b>beautiful</b>\" - Hebrew literally \"<b>good</b>\" child</li><li class=\"d1\">idea: <b>healthy</b>, \"a healthy everything child\"</li><li class=\"d1\">they think \"uh-oh\" - <b>open season on boy babies</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Hidden <b>three months</b>; can't hide longer</li><li class=\"d0\">Basket = <b>ark of bulrushes</b>, daubed with <b>asphalt</b> (tar/pitch)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"she's not paving a street\"</li><li class=\"d1\">laid in the <b>reeds</b> by the river bank</li><li class=\"d0\">Echo: an <b>ark</b> sealed with pitch → \"think back to <b>Noah</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Sister watches from a distance</li><li class=\"d1\">baby making noise - eventually <b>found and potentially killed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":109.3,"lv":2,"h":"2:5-10 - Pharaoh's daughter","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Pharaoh's daughter</b> comes to bathe; maid fetches the ark</li><li class=\"d0\">Opens it: child crying; <b>compassion</b></li><li class=\"d0\">She knows it's a <b>Hebrew</b> baby</li><li class=\"d1\">how? <b>circumcision</b> - easy giveaway</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this is going to become an issue later with Moses\"</li><li class=\"d0\">She has \"some kind of decency\" - <b>won't drown it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sister offers a <b>Hebrew nurse</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Pharaoh's daughter will adopt but <b>not lactating</b> - needs <b>wet nurse</b></li><li class=\"d1\">girl doesn't reveal relation - fetches <b>Moses' own mother</b></li><li class=\"d1\">mother paid to nurse her own son</li><li class=\"d0\">Boy grown → brought to Pharaoh's daughter; <b>becomes her son</b></li></ul>"},{"t":224.69,"lv":3,"h":"The name \"Moses\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Because I <b>drew him out</b> of the water\" - Hebrew etymology</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Moses</b> = \"<b>child</b>\" in <b>Egyptian</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in Hebrew sounds close to \"<b>drew out</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>wordplay</b> - text includes both</li></ul>"},{"t":271.6,"lv":3,"h":"Was there an Egyptian god in his name?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Unresolvable question: was \"Moses\" his <b>whole name</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">maybe an <b>Egyptian god's name</b> in front, dropped once he became prophet of <b>Yahweh</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't prove either way; plausible to give, plausible to drop</li><li class=\"d0\">He leans to: <b>no</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sister <b>Miriam</b> - Egyptian name, \"<b>beloved of Amun</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">pagan god in her name <b>not removed</b> in the rest of the Pentateuch</li><li class=\"d1\">so Moses' wouldn't have been removed either</li></ul>"},{"t":369.24,"lv":2,"h":"Egyptian names among the Levites","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Aaron</b> also an Egyptian name</li><li class=\"d0\">Why would non-adopted kids have Egyptian names?</li><li class=\"d1\">assimilation? parents wouldn't have pushed it - \"nothing saying they did\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not just this family: \"a whole pile of the <b>Levites</b>\" have Egyptian names</li><li class=\"d0\">Theme through the <b>rest of the Pentateuch</b>; first place we see it</li></ul>"},{"t":421.17,"lv":2,"h":"Early Israel not ethnically united","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Future nation <b>not ethnically united</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not all <b>biologically descended from Abraham</b>; some are, many aren't</li><li class=\"d1\">not how <b>tribe and clan identity</b> worked</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient world: no <b>DNA</b>, \"no <b>23 and Me</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">couldn't even prove <b>paternity</b> of immediate father, let alone great-great-grandfather</li><li class=\"d0\">Identity through <b>rituals</b> of the clan</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>what God</b> they worshipped; participating in the family/clan/tribal unit</li><li class=\"d1\">could <b>marry in</b> or become a <b>servant</b> → part of household</li><li class=\"d1\">servants in a Levite household <b>considered Levites</b>, whatever their \"ethnicity\"</li></ul>"},{"t":514.67,"lv":3,"h":"Joseph, and the third high priest","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">First tease: <b>Joseph</b> married daughter of an <b>Egyptian priest</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Must get \"ethnic Israel\" out of our heads - \"not what the Torah says\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Will get \"more and more extreme examples\"</li><li class=\"d1\">tease: <b>third high priest</b> of Israel is <b>black</b> - literally an African</li><li class=\"d0\">We think of Jewish as ethnicity → assume original Israel = descendants</li><li class=\"d1\">some true; some <b>converted along the way</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Once in the land: Israelites marry Israelites</li><li class=\"d1\">village marries village; after <b>5-7 generations</b> an ethnic Israelite exists</li><li class=\"d1\">but nascent Israel = \"<b>ethnically diverse</b> in our modern terms\"</li></ul>"},{"t":618.96,"lv":3,"h":"Why it matters; early church parallel","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">False narrative: OT salvation = <b>ethnic</b>, NT = not</li><li class=\"d1\">\"the truth is it <b>never was</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Early church</b> parallel</li><li class=\"d1\">ethnically diverse at first; then Christians marry Christians - not pagans, later not Muslims</li><li class=\"d1\">churches in places become <b>more ethnically uniform</b> than when <b>St. Paul</b> founded them</li><li class=\"d0\">Same for Israel: <b>diverse at first layer</b></li><li class=\"d1\">maybe even <b>Moses, Aaron, Miriam</b> ethnically Egyptian</li><li class=\"d1\">but family part of a clan in <b>tribe of Levi</b> → <b>Levites, Hebrews, Israelites</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't matter what color their skin is\"</li></ul>"},{"t":728.95,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: why suspicious of them as Asiatics?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Why still suspicion of them as \"<b>Asiatics</b>\" - not ritually participating?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no citizenship</b>; loyalties to <b>family, clan, tribe, kin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">closer kinship (ritual etc.) to <b>Edomites</b>, <b>Midianites</b> who worship same God</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: Egyptian names handed down from great-grandfather?</li><li class=\"d1\">or an Egyptian version of your name; or a slave in an Egyptian household</li><li class=\"d0\">Way of life now: <b>slavery</b></li><li class=\"d1\">rest of Torah lays out how to <b>eat, dress, conduct themselves</b>, <b>feasts</b>, <b>sacrifices</b> - not received yet</li><li class=\"d1\">so not as radical a difference from Egyptian life as later; no <b>antipathy</b> yet</li></ul>"},{"t":838.69,"lv":3,"h":"Foreign wives, Joseph and Asenath","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Later view: <b>Ezra and Nehemiah</b> - \"can't have foreign wives\"</li><li class=\"d1\">even in Genesis: <b>Esau</b> married Canaanites - big problem</li><li class=\"d1\">yet text casually says <b>Joseph</b> marries daughter of pagan priest</li><li class=\"d1\">stories of <b>Joseph and Asenath</b> grew out of \"wait, what?\" - her conversion</li><li class=\"d0\">People <b>not yet called out of Egypt</b></li><li class=\"d1\">worship a particular God, not Egyptian gods</li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>Laban</b>, <b>Judah</b>, some of Jacob's sons took part in other things</li><li class=\"d0\">Egyptian worry = <b>loyalties</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fight for Egypt or for <b>cousins from the East</b>? \"I'm worried it's... their cousins\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not yet \"wearing <b>tassels</b>\" on garments</li></ul>"},{"t":959.23,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: how did tribal identity survive?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: How keep tribal identity tied to <b>sons of Jacob</b>, enslaved, distant memory?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>continuity of family traditions</b>; telling stories of the ancestor</li><li class=\"d1\">no way to check \"is he really our ancestor?\" - \"part of the unit now\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>oral tradition</b> at this point</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: <b>America</b> and the 13 colonies</li><li class=\"d1\">descended from all kinds of people but adopt the <b>founding fathers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">family lore of a Native American ancestor - passed orally, genetically unknown</li><li class=\"d0\">Recall <b>altars</b> and patriarchal narratives</li><li class=\"d1\">traditions of each group about <b>their piece of land</b>, forefather's altar there</li><li class=\"d1\">brought together later; <b>not yet</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Midianites, Edomites, Ishmaelites, Moabites, Ammonites: \"<b>Abraham's our ancestor too</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1096.96,"lv":3,"h":"Paul in 1 Corinthians: \"our fathers\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same in <b>NT</b> - <b>1 Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul speaking to <b>Gentiles</b>, ex-pagans: \"our fathers went through the <b>Red Sea</b> with Moses\"</li><li class=\"d1\">few Greeks in Corinth had ancestors there - \"those are their fathers now\" because <b>Christians</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Galatians, Romans</b>: Abraham your father <b>if faithful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">unfaithful: doesn't matter \"<b>according to the flesh</b>\" (we'd say genetically)</li><li class=\"d1\">what makes you descendant = <b>being faithful like he was</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1179.28,"lv":2,"h":"Exodus 2:11-12 - Moses kills an Egyptian","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses now adopted, growing up in <b>Pharaoh's extended household</b></li><li class=\"d1\">life \"very different than the other Israelites\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Goes out to <b>brethren</b>, sees burdens; Egyptian beating a Hebrew</li><li class=\"d0\">Looks this way and that; kills him, <b>hides body in sand</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First look at his <b>personality</b>: \"may have some <b>anger management issues</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">plays out later in his life</li><li class=\"d0\">Big problem? He's Pharaoh's adopted grandson - could get away with killing a rando</li><li class=\"d1\">real problem: <b>loyalty</b> - killed an Egyptian to protect one of these <b>Asiatics</b></li><li class=\"d1\">loyal to Pharaoh who raised him, or to <b>these other people</b>?</li></ul>"},{"t":1288.03,"lv":3,"h":"2:13-15 - Found out, flees","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Second day: <b>two Hebrews fighting</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Why are you hitting your <b>neighbor</b>?\" - fellow Israelite</li><li class=\"d0\">Reply: \"Who made you a <b>prince and judge</b> over us? Kill me like the Egyptian?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses alarmed: \"Surely this thing is known\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"shallow grave\" - why do murderers always bury in shallow graves?</li><li class=\"d0\">v. 15: Pharaoh hears, <b>seeks to kill Moses</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Moses flees east into desert, <b>land of Midian</b>, sits by a <b>well</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1377.54,"lv":3,"h":"Moses' life in three 40s; his education","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Moses lives to <b>120</b>; life in <b>four periods of 40</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nursed until \"grown\"/weaned - vague; but he's <b>40</b> here</li><li class=\"d0\">In the ancient world adult at puberty (<b>13-14</b>) → decent adulthood under his belt</li><li class=\"d0\">Educated in the ways of the Egyptians</li><li class=\"d1\">literate in <b>hieratic</b>; Pharaoh's court corresponded with the east on treaties, trade</li><li class=\"d1\">letters on <b>cuneiform tablets</b> → likely knew those and eastern traditions</li><li class=\"d1\">not India or Europe</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Highly educated adult for this time and place\" - now alone in the desert</li></ul>"},{"t":1509.97,"lv":2,"h":"Midian: desert nomads","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Midianites</b> = where they live; basically the <b>Sinai</b> desert</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Bedouin</b> nomads</li><li class=\"d1\">trace forefathers to <b>Ishmael</b> or <b>Edom/Esau</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cousins of Israelites, nomadic like <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jacob took family to Egypt - no other Abrahamite relatives went</li><li class=\"d1\">more on them in <b>Deuteronomy 2</b></li><li class=\"d0\">In the desert \"it behooves you to find a <b>well</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1589.2,"lv":2,"h":"Exodus 2:16-17 - The well and Jethro","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Priest of Midian</b>, <b>seven daughters</b>, flock of father <b>Jethro</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Daughters draw water, fill troughs; <b>shepherds drive them off</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Moses stands up, waters the flock</li><li class=\"d1\">nobody owns this well - built around a spring; whoever finds it uses it</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses \"goes all <b>Boy Scout astronaut</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1624.46,"lv":3,"h":"Jethro as a priest","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Also called by another name - more in <b>Numbers</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jethro</b> may be a <b>title</b> for priests among these tribes</li><li class=\"d0\">Priest not with a temple - nomads</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b> building altars, sacrificing</li><li class=\"d1\">for extended family; others from <b>other clans</b> came to supervise <b>feasts</b> with sacrifices to their God</li></ul>"},{"t":1724.55,"lv":3,"h":"Echo of Rebecca at the well","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Does the scene remind you of anybody?</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Abraham's servant</b> fetching <b>Rebecca</b> for Isaac - well, helped them</li><li class=\"d1\">\"echoes again\" of this imagery</li></ul>"},{"t":1742.28,"lv":3,"h":"2:18-22 - Hospitality, Zipporah, Gershom","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Father asks \"how come so soon?\"; \"<b>An Egyptian</b> delivered us\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Jethro = head of household; daughters maybe <b>granddaughters</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the father here is their immediate father</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Why have you left the man? Call him to <b>eat bread</b>\" - \"hospitality, hello\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses dwells there; gives <b>Zipporah</b>; son <b>Gershom</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"big time lapse\" - dinner, stays, joins household, marries, son</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>gur</i> = foreigner/<b>sojourner</b> - \"resident alien\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>sojourner in a foreign land</b>\" - outsider as Egyptian in desert or in desert? \"probably both\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1855.0,"lv":2,"h":"2:23-25 - God remembers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>King of Egypt dies</b>; Israel groans, cries out; <b>God heard</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>remembered his covenant</b> with Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</li><li class=\"d1\">another <b>time lapse</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Moses spends <b>second 40 years</b> with Jethro</li><li class=\"d1\">sent back at <b>80</b> - \"spoilers\"</li><li class=\"d1\">story of Moses, <b>Exodus 3 to end of Deuteronomy</b> = age <b>80 to 120</b></li><li class=\"d1\">two lives already: Egypt, then nomad shepherd</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: first time they prayed? \"No, obviously not\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"remembered\" ≠ forgot; means <b>brought to mind</b> - he now <b>reveals himself</b></li><li class=\"d1\">time to act: promises become <b>present reality</b>, not just hope or oral memory</li></ul>"},{"t":2036.06,"lv":3,"h":"A God who hadn't appeared","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Genesis traditions: God <b>appeared in person</b> to Abraham - walked, talked, ate</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Isaac</b>, <b>Jacob</b> - multiple appearances</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Joseph</b>: only <b>dreams</b> - \"a level of remove\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Now: no appearances, no dreams or visions, <b>no prophets yet</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Part of why God acts: <b>faithfulness of Israelites</b> in keeping traditions</li><li class=\"d1\">but not the only ones: <b>Midianites</b> too - Jethro sacrifices to the <b>God of Abraham</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2129.81,"lv":2,"h":"Abraham's promises: nations, plural","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Another category to see in the Torah, beside ethnic diversity</li><li class=\"d0\">Promise: \"<b>nations</b>, plural, will come from you\"; all nations blessed</li><li class=\"d1\">wrongly taught: single line (<b>Isaac, Jacob, Judah, David</b>, Messiah) = only the saved</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two elements</b>: singular line to <b>Messiah</b>; collective broader <b>descendants</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Esau</b> not the line - not evil: \"arguably a better person than Jacob\"</li><li class=\"d1\">more forgiving, less conniving</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Edomites, Ishmaelites, Midianites</b> not cut off - part of <b>Abraham's inheritance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Get rid of the bad category: salvation for ethnic Israelites / one line only</li></ul>"},{"t":2285.2,"lv":2,"h":"Next: burning bush; where was God?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Stopping here - no time for chapter 3</li><li class=\"d0\">God reveals himself on the <b>holy mountain</b> of the Midianites - \"still out there worshiping him\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Moses encounters God, then <b>returns to Egypt</b> to represent God to Israel</li><li class=\"d0\">Tradition through the whole <b>Hebrew Bible</b>: where is God while Israel in Egypt?</li><li class=\"d1\">answer: <b>Edom</b>, <b>Mount Seir</b></li><li class=\"d1\">worshipped there; comes from there to get Israel to <b>Canaan</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Next time: the <b>burning bush</b></li></ul>"}],"next":"Next week: <b>burning bush</b>, Exodus 3"});
